 client instance follows the [SSH config file](https://www.digitalocean.com/community/tutorials/how-to-configure-custom-connection-options-for-your-ssh-client)
 standards, but the interface can be replaced so as to implement whatever proprietary logic is required.
 
+The config file is evaluated the way OpenSSH evaluates it: `Host` and `Match` blocks apply in file order and the first
+value obtained for a keyword wins. `Match` supports the `all`, `host`, `originalhost`, `user` and `final` criteria;
+`exec`, `localnetwork`, `localuser` and `canonical` are not supported. `Include` files are read in place when a host is
+resolved, so edits to them take effect on the next connection; relative `Include` paths are taken from the `.ssh`
+folder of the user's home (see `PathUtils#getUserHomeFolder`). Unlike OpenSSH, the permissions of included files are
+not checked. Host names and patterns are compared without regard to case; the `HostName` keeps the case it was given,
+as before, unless a `Match final` makes a second pass, which fixes the resolved name in lower case as OpenSSH does.
+
